An "Application" is any work that makes use of an interface provided
by the Library, but which is not otherwise based on the Library.
Defining a class by inheriting from a class defined in the Library is
considered a mode of using an interface provided by the Library.

A "Combined Work" is a work produced by combining or linking an
Application with the Library.  The particular version of the Library
with which the Combined Work was made is also called the "Linked
Version".

The "Minimal Corresponding Source" for a Combined Work means the
Corresponding Source for the Combined Work, excluding any source code
for portions of the Combined Work that, considered in isolation, are based
on the Application, and not on the Linked Version.

The "Corresponding Application Code" for a Combined Work means the
object code and/or source code for the Application, including any data
and utility programs needed for reproducing the Combined Work from the
Corresponding Source, but excluding the System Libraries of the
Combined Work.

1. Exception to Section 3 of the GNU GPL.

You may convey a covered work in object code form under the terms of
sections 4 and 5 of the GNU GPL provided that you also convey the
machine-readable Corresponding Source under the terms of this License,
in one of the following ways:

a) Convey the Corresponding Source in the same file or files as
the object code, under the terms of section 4 of the GNU GPL,
provided that you also convey the machine-readable Corresponding
Source.

b) Convey the Corresponding Source in a physical product,
including a physical storage medium, accompanied by the Corresponding
Source fixed on a durable physical medium customarily used for
software interchange.

c) Convey the Corresponding Source using a network server,
provided that you make the Corresponding Source available from
that server at no extra charge to anyone using the network server
to obtain the object code.

d) Convey the Corresponding Source by offering access from a
designated place (gratis or for a charge), and offer equivalent
access to the Corresponding Source in the same way through the same
place at no further charge.  You need not require recipients to
copy the Corresponding Source along with the object code.  If the
place to copy the object code is a network server, the
Corresponding Source may be on a different server (operated by you
or a third party) that supports equivalent copying facilities,
provided you maintain clear directions next to the object code saying
where to find the Corresponding Source.  You need not require a
recipient to download the Corresponding Source separately from the
object code.

e) Convey the Corresponding Source using a peer-to-peer network,
provided you inform other peers where the Corresponding Source and
Corresponding Application Code of the work are available and offer no
impediment to obtaining them in the same way that you provide
access to the object code, and provided that you do not keep a
copy of the Corresponding Source or Corresponding Application Code
of the work on a network server that is used to deliver the
object code to the peer.

A separable portion of the object code, whose source code is excluded
from the Corresponding Source as a System Library, need not be
included in conveying the Corresponding Source.

